1
00:00:00,919 --> 00:00:08,895
Welcome to the Africa capitals, episode four of six. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,548 --> 00:00:16,154
We turn to the Horn of Africa at the Red Sea, then inland to the great lakes
of the equator.

3
00:00:16,861 --> 00:00:19,415
Let's bring Djibouti into view.

4
00:00:25,354 --> 00:00:34,735
Djibouti stands at the head of the Gulf of Tadjoura, on the Bab-el-Mandeb
strait, in the small country the map names Djibouti.

5
00:00:35,399 --> 00:00:45,802
A railway runs from the Ethiopian highlands down to this harbour, and the Lake
Assal depression below sea level, with its salt crust, lies close by.

6
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:53,066
Remember the harbour at the strait, where the rail line from inland Ethiopia
comes down to the sea.

7
00:00:53,768 --> 00:00:55,765
Let's bring Asmara into view.

8
00:01:01,466 --> 00:01:08,583
Asmara stands in the Eritrean highlands, well inland from the long Red Sea
coast below it.

9
00:01:09,243 --> 00:01:19,866
The capital preserves an exceptional group of early modernist buildings from
Italian colonial rule, inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2017.

10
00:01:20,524 --> 00:01:26,538
Remember the modernist highland capital, high above Eritrea’s Red Sea coast.

11
00:01:27,231 --> 00:01:30,644
Let's bring Addis Ababa into view.

12
00:01:35,044 --> 00:01:42,289
Addis Ababa stands on the Ethiopian highland plateau, west of the Horn of
Africa and south of Eritrea.

13
00:01:42,948 --> 00:01:52,793
The national museum keeps the Middle Awash fossils, including the famous
“Lucy” specimen, and the city’s name means “new flower”.

14
00:01:53,442 --> 00:02:02,486
Remember the new flower at 2,355 metres, the highland capital whose national
museum holds Lucy.

15
00:02:03,182 --> 00:02:05,783
Let's bring Mogadishu into view.

16
00:02:10,799 --> 00:02:17,753
Mogadishu stands on the Indian Ocean, in the south of Somalia, at the bottom
of the Horn.

17
00:02:18,419 --> 00:02:30,401
One of the oldest trading cities of East Africa, Ibn Battuta found it rich
when he visited in 1331, and its Arba’a Rukun mosque dates from 1269.

18
00:02:31,063 --> 00:02:39,074
Remember the Indian Ocean harbour at the foot of the Horn, where a traveller
found a rich trading city in 1331.

19
00:02:39,763 --> 00:02:41,725
Let's bring Hargeisa into view.

20
00:02:46,937 --> 00:02:58,791
Hargeisa is the capital of Somaliland, a self-declared state that the United
Nations treats as part of Somalia; Somalia’s own capital is Mogadishu.

21
00:02:59,441 --> 00:03:16,159
It was largely destroyed in the war of 1988 and rebuilt as the seat of
Somaliland’s government; its camel market is now one of the largest in the
Horn of Africa.

22
00:03:16,816 --> 00:03:25,860
Remember the two Somali capitals: Hargeisa in the northern interior, Mogadishu
on the Indian Ocean in the south.

23
00:03:26,541 --> 00:03:28,538
Let's bring Kampala into view.

24
00:03:33,428 --> 00:03:40,893
Kampala stands on the hills above Lake Victoria in Uganda, west of Kenya.

25
00:03:41,548 --> 00:03:52,636
The Kasubi Tombs on one of those hills hold the burial places of the Kabaka
kings, and were inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2001.

26
00:03:53,297 --> 00:03:57,384
Remember the royal tombs on the hills above Lake Victoria.

27
00:03:58,056 --> 00:04:00,401
Let's bring Nairobi into view.

28
00:04:04,936 --> 00:04:12,691
Nairobi stands in Kenya, inland from the Indian Ocean, on the high ground
above the Rift Valley lakes.

29
00:04:13,349 --> 00:04:31,061
It began in 1899 as a depot on the railway from Mombasa to Uganda, and today a
national park with lions and giraffes lies in sight of its tower blocks; its
name is often read from a Maasai phrase for “cool water”.

30
00:04:31,712 --> 00:04:38,399
Remember the “cool water” capital: the railway depot that grew up between a
national park and the highlands.

31
00:04:39,086 --> 00:04:41,257
Let's bring Kigali into view.

32
00:04:46,132 --> 00:04:52,401
Kigali stands in the hills of Rwanda, north of Burundi and west of Tanzania.

33
00:04:53,058 --> 00:05:04,273
The memorial at Gisozi keeps the memory of the 1994 genocide against the
Tutsi, and the capital has since become one of the greenest cities in Africa.

34
00:05:04,923 --> 00:05:11,018
Remember the green hills of Rwanda, and the memorial standing above the
capital.

35
00:05:13,273 --> 00:05:20,390
Gitega stands in the centre of Burundi, inland from the Lake Tanganyika shore,
in the middle of the country.

36
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:31,325
It is the old royal town, and it replaced Bujumbura as the capital in 2019;
the royal drum sanctuary of Gishora is close by.

37
00:05:31,989 --> 00:05:38,723
Remember the inland centre that took over from the lakeside capital, with
Rwanda just to the north.

38
00:05:39,511 --> 00:05:42,402
Let's bring Dodoma into view.

39
00:05:53,148 --> 00:05:59,499
Dodoma stands inland in central Tanzania, south of Kenya and west of the
Indian Ocean coast.

40
00:06:00,165 --> 00:06:12,019
It was chosen as the capital in 1973, near the middle of the country so that
the seat of government would be close to every region, and the State House
stands on a hill above the town.

41
00:06:12,683 --> 00:06:20,148
Remember the inland capital chosen in the middle of Tanzania, below Kenya and
far from the sea.

42
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:46,778
Djibouti guards the strait where the railway from inland Ethiopia reaches the
sea, Asmara keeps its modernist highland buildings, Addis Ababa holds Lucy’s
country, and Mogadishu faces the Indian Ocean.

43
00:06:47,432 --> 00:07:09,896
Remember the two Somali capitals, Hargeisa inland in the north and Mogadishu
on the ocean in the south, then the royal tombs above Lake Victoria at
Kampala, the railway capital and its park at Nairobi, the green hills at
Kigali, the inland centre at Gitega and the middle-of-Tanzania capital at
Dodoma.
